# CASP15 H1114 — The Enzyme That Eats Air (7UTD)
---

This is one of the most remarkable enzymes ever discovered.

It's called Huc — a hydrogenase from Mycobacterium smegmatis
that can extract energy from the trace amounts of hydrogen
in ordinary air.

Published in Nature in 2023 by Grinter and colleagues.

> close
> load 7utd
---

Look at this massive complex — over a thousand residues
arranged in a huge oligomeric assembly.

This is the machine that lets soil bacteria survive
when there's nothing else to eat. They literally
breathe hydrogen from the atmosphere.

> spin 0.15
---

Now let's zoom into where the magic happens —
the active site with its nickel-iron cofactor.

This NiFe cluster is where hydrogen gas is split
into protons and electrons, generating energy
from a gas that makes up less than one part
per million of our atmosphere.

> stop
> view ligand
---

The fact that CASP15 included this target shows
how challenging protein structure prediction has become.

With its complex oligomeric state and metal cofactors,
this was one of the hardest targets in the competition.

> reset
> spin 0.1
